{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>From twenty years old.</b> One needs to know why it does not mention the number over which the phrase ״from twenty years old״ ranges, and if it relies on the number mentioned in the adjacent passage — as one says regarding something mentioned nearby — then the phrase ״from twenty years old״ etc. should likewise have relied on what was mentioned nearby and not needed to be stated again. And according to what I explained in the preceding verse — that the count in the plains of Moab came also to show that they did not diminish through it — the matter would logically require counting the minors as well, for without this there is no praise that they did not diminish, for they always diminished except that the kids grew and became he-goats, and one might say that all of them were counted; therefore it says ״from twenty years old,״ and Scripture gives a reason for the matter: <b>as HaShem commanded.</b> For if the matter had been on Moses' part alone — to return the flock by count, and the word of HaShem had been only to command him concerning the matter, as it is written: ״Take the head״ etc. — Moses would not have counted Israel on his own initiative even if he had a reason for it; therefore he counted only from twenty years old, as HaShem commanded, etc." "<b>And the children of Israel who came out of the land of Egypt.</b> The intention of this phrase is not known. And Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said that it means there were among those counted many from those who came out of Egypt. But there is no reason why Scripture should inform us of this matter, and furthermore I certainly know that there were among them some who came out of Egypt, since the decree was decreed only upon those who came out from twenty years old. Perhaps the intention of Scripture is to inform you that the children of Israel were counted and found to be the same number as those who came out of the land of Egypt — not like the number in the portion of Bamidbar, where they were six hundred thousand and three thousand and five hundred and fifty, whereas this number rose to six hundred and one thousand and seven hundred and thirty, which fell short of the number in Bamidbar. And Moses was under no obligation except to return by count those who had been deposited with him at the beginning, and behold, their number was complete. And the construal of the verse is in this manner: ״And the children of Israel״ — their count rose to the count of those who came out, etc., and not below it. Or it may intend in this manner: their number was arranged according to the order of the number of those who came out of the land of Egypt, which was counted by families, as it is written (Genesis 46:9): ״the sons of Reuben: Hanoch and Pallu, the sons of Simeon: Jemuel and Jamin״ etc.; likewise this number was counted by families as recorded in the matter, and not in the manner they were counted in the wilderness of Sinai, where the details of the families were not mentioned there."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:41.329001106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}